PG0 called this the most dangerous finding in the audit, and it is the only
one where the instrument itself was lying.

WHAT WAS BROKEN

Two exits out of the floor designer, each assembling its own URL, each
broken in a different way:

  · /design/spec built `/#quote?spec=…` — the query string placed AFTER the
    fragment, so the browser treats the whole thing as a hash and the
    destination never sees a parameter. Nothing threw. Nothing turned red.
  · /design's primary CTA was the bare anchor `/#quote`, carrying nothing at
    all, landing on a homepage section whose form reads localStorage.

And both fired `design_handoff` on click, unconditionally. So the funnel
recorded a successful handoff every time one failed. A metric that fails in
the optimistic direction is worse than no metric, because it is acted on.

WHAT I GOT WRONG ON THE WAY, AND CORRECTED

My first draft of the docblock said /design "never wrote to its own
querystring". False — FloorConfigurator's persistence effect mirrors the
configuration into the address bar, so the design PAGE is genuinely
shareable and reload-proof. The HANDOFF was not. A link opened on a
spouse's phone showed the right floor and then dropped every bit of it at
the quote form. The comment now says that, because shipping a false claim
inside a patch about false claims would be its own joke.

THE FIX

`designEstimateHref` is now the one place either exit is built, targeting
/estimate — a real page with a real form that reads its parameters —
instead of a homepage anchor that parses none. Query before fragment, with
a test that asserts the ordering by parsing the href the way a browser
does.

`design_handoff` now carries `carried:`, so the event reports whether the
configuration actually travelled rather than merely that someone clicked.

MEAS-01's join key reaches the second configurator. Without it, leads from
/design stay unattributable while Floor Studio's are attributable — which
would have made Floor Studio look like the only tool that converts, purely
because it was the only one that could be counted.

ONE DESIGN, ONE ID

`saveStudioDesign` mirrors a studio design into `ew-design-v1`. That mirror
now passes the studio's OWN id rather than letting the mirror mint a
second. Two ids for one design means the studio reports it under one key
and a later /design edit reports it under the other: one customer, counted
twice. A test asserts the line.

`designConfigFromParams` refuses a half-configuration — species and a
positive area or nothing — because a form prefilled with half an answer is
worse than an empty one: the visitor cannot tell which half came from them.
And the estimate form now prefers a configuration in the LINK over whatever
this browser last stored, the rule Floor Studio already followed.

THE TEST THAT FAILED FIRST

"Neither file may hand-build a quote link" failed immediately — on the new
DOCBLOCK, which quotes the broken URL to explain it. A test that reads raw
source cannot tell an explanation from the thing it explains, and a test
that forbids its own documentation is a test that will be deleted. It now
strips comments and asserts against code.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards (verify:css needs postcss from node_modules,
absent here). parse-scan 606 files, zero diagnostics. Every changed file
through a syntax-only tsc pass — no TS1xxx, no TS2448. 36 new assertions;
68,175 across the local suite. The 40 remaining failures are wood.test.ts's
it.each under my local shim, pre-existing and unrelated — verified
identical on a clean tree.

